R=ROOT/'revision/review_v4'
s=(R/'manuscript_working.md').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
def replace(start,end,new):
 global s
 a=s.index(start+'\n\n')+len(start)+2;b=s.index('\n\n'+end,a);s=s[:a]+new.strip()+s[b:]

s=s.replace(s.splitlines()[0],'# Climate information and coherent combinations of subseasonal rainfall forecasts over Australia',1)
abstract='''Climate information can improve rainfall calibration without establishing an additional benefit from temporal reconciliation. We examine these contributions using weekly and aggregate ECMWF hindcasts over Australia. A controlled experiment separates aggregate climate predictors from hierarchy size, with nested year-block validation and adjacent-year exclusion. Four primary domains are followed by four geographical extensions and a northern ACCESS-S2 check. We also combine coherent raw, weekly-aggregation and reconciled distributions using constant, seasonal or state-dependent weights learned from inner held-out forecasts. None of the original primary comparisons passes multiplicity adjustment. The northern ECMWF climate effect is exploratory and concentrated in a few years; ACCESS-S2 does not reproduce a clear climate contrast. In the northern forward evaluation, raw ECMWF outperforms the individual calibrated alternatives. State-dependent mixing improves on climate-informed weekly aggregation by 1.22% of raw CRPS across primary domains (95% interval 0.33–2.03%), but does not improve on constant mixing. Covariance-based diagnostics overpredict reconciliation gains in every primary domain-season case. Additional geographical results are mixed when climate-informed reconciliation is compared with its rainfall-only counterpart. These findings separate the value of climate information, aggregation and forecast combination. Simple coherent alternatives remain competitive, and increasing the flexibility of forecast weights does not establish a more reliable selection rule.'''
replace('## Abstract','Keywords: Australia; subseasonal prediction; precipitation; temporal reconciliation; climate indices; probabilistic calibration; ensemble dependence; forecast verification.',abstract)

old='The study contributes a controlled comparison of these information pathways, a diagnosis of when covariance-based gain estimates overstate realised improvement, and an assessment of the sensitivity to evaluation years and forecast support. The aim is to identify conditions under which added complexity is useful. The main experiment retains its original primary comparisons; subsequent controls and transfer checks are reported as exploratory analyses.'
new='''A further question follows when the raw ensemble or weekly aggregation performs better than reconciliation: can information available at forecast initialisation identify the more useful distribution? Probability pooling is established, and its effects on calibration require evaluation (Ranjan and Gneiting 2010). Recent machine-learning postprocessing also targets subseasonal ensemble probabilities (Guan et al. 2026). Here we test a narrower question by mixing complete coherent rainfall trajectories and comparing state-dependent weights with constant weights and simple inner-score selection. This makes forecast combination a controlled extension, rather than treating a flexible mixture as a new reconciliation theory.

The study contributes a separation of climate-information and reconciliation effects, an evaluation of forecast selection and combination, and transfer checks across geographical supports and forecast systems. The original factorial comparisons retain their primary status. Subsequent controls, mixtures and transfer analyses address questions raised by the original results and are reported as extensions. Their dates and the results already inspected at each stage are recorded in the analysis plans.'''
s=s.replace(old,new)
access='''The second-system check uses public ACCESS-S2 research hindcasts from NCI (Wedd et al. 2022; Australian Bureau of Meteorology, undated a). We retain the first day of each month during 2000–2018, nine members and the first 28 daily rainfall fields, giving 228 origins in the N domain. The downloaded fluxes, in kilograms per square metre per second, are converted to daily millimetres. Times mark daily midpoints; the files identify daily means but omit the referenced time-bounds variable. We interpret these as UTC calendar-day means and retain the source metadata. Exact overlap and latitude-area weights average the forecast cells within N. AGCD uses the same land-domain observation support. The unchanged calibration settings are fitted separately to this system. Its monthly origins, grid and member count differ from ECMWF, so the analysis compares methods within each system and does not rank the systems by their absolute scores.'''
s=s.replace('### 2.3 Climate predictors',access+'\n\n### 2.3 Climate predictors',1)
s=s.replace('It contains 217 to 252 origins in the outer fits.','For ECMWF it contains 217 to 252 origins in the outer fits; the monthly ACCESS-S2 sample is smaller.')
s=s.replace('Each outer fold consequently contains 897 to 954 training origins and 53 validation origins.','Each ECMWF outer fold consequently contains 897 to 954 training origins and 53 validation origins. ACCESS-S2 applies the same exclusion rule to its nineteen years, with twelve validation origins per fold.')
s=s.replace('no index is selected because of its verification score.','the reported single-index controls do not change the full-model predictor set.')

mixture='''### 3.6 Coherent mixtures and forecast selection

For a forecast origin t, let P₁, P₂ and P₃ denote the joint distributions of the raw ensemble, climate-informed weekly aggregation and seven-node H1. Every sampled trajectory has nonnegative weeks and aggregates equal to their sums. We form a linear probability pool:

[[EQ16]]

The same weights apply to all nodes of a trajectory, so the mixture remains coherent. This operation selects a whole trajectory from a component distribution; it does not average independently sampled weekly amounts. Constant, seasonal and state-dependent softmax models use two coefficient vectors, with the H1 logit fixed at zero for identification. Seasonal features are annual sine and cosine. State features add lagged RMM1, RMM2 and DMI, log raw total spread, and the difference between the H1 and weekly-aggregation mean totals. The raw ensemble itself has zero aggregation discrepancy. Equal component weights provide a further control.

Weights minimise inner out-of-fold 28-day empirical CRPS scaled by mean training rainfall, with a fixed 0.01 penalty on the mean squared coefficients. Inner years receive equal total weight, and standardisation uses only their records. Covariance estimation and dependence templates are also fitted inside each inner training set. The score of the weighted empirical mixture is evaluated exactly:

[[EQ17]]

Here Aⱼ is the mean absolute distance between component j and the observation; Bⱼₖ is the mean pairwise absolute distance between components j and k, including within-component pairs. This evaluates the delivered empirical distributions at their actual sizes without a stochastic resampling step. Coefficient limits, gradients and numerical checks are documented in Supplementary Note 3. A separate rule chooses the lowest inner-CRPS forecast among raw, BU-rain, BU-clim, H0 and H1, either globally or by season. All fitted choices are then applied to outer observations once.

The two principal mixture-extension contrasts are state-dependent versus constant weights and state-dependent versus BU-clim. Domain differences are divided by each domain's raw CRPS and averaged equally. Common calendar-year resampling preserves dependence across domains; Holm adjustment covers these two extension tests. They are separate from the original factorial family. The retrospective forward check fixes training through 2014, and three rolling checks fix cut-offs at 2009, 2012 and 2015, each verifying the following three years. These periods overlap the analysed archive and are not a new operational holdout. For decision diagnostics, training-only terciles define ranked probability skill scores, while fixed 50 and 100 mm totals define Brier scores and idealised cost–loss curves. These are generic rainfall thresholds, not crop-specific loss models (Supplementary Tables S19–S21).'''
s=s.replace('## 4 Results',mixture+'\n\n## 4 Results',1)
# Correct the supplementary map before inserting new sections.
s=s.replace('Supplementary Tables S1, S5 and S14','Supplementary Tables S1 and S5').replace('diagnostics without model refitting (Supplementary Table S15)','diagnostics without model refitting (Supplementary Table S14)').replace('scores (Supplementary Table S16)','scores (Supplementary Table S15)')
s=s.replace('Three fixed permutations are diagnostic controls and do not constitute a permutation test.','The original three training-only permutations are retained as diagnostic controls. A new set of twenty full year-block permutations shifts the joint climate series across origin years, refits every nested fold and evaluates the complete shuffled predictor stream. Its Monte Carlo p value assumes exchangeable year blocks; climate persistence makes this an approximate sensitivity test.')
pl=json.loads((R/'placebo/summary.json').read_text())
s=s.replace('These controls do not reproduce the improvement, but three fitted permutations cannot quantify a null distribution.',f"These controls do not reproduce the improvement. In the twenty full year-block permutations, CRPS ranges from {pl['null_min']:.3f} to {pl['null_max']:.3f} mm; none is below the unpermuted {pl['observed']:.3f} mm, giving a one-sided Monte Carlo p value of {pl['monte_carlo_p']:.3f}. The result is conditional on approximate year-block exchangeability and does not identify the responsible climate index (Supplementary Table S18).")
s=s.replace('(Table 6). These controls','(Supplementary Table S18). These controls')
s=s.replace('### 4.7 Added-information controls and forward evaluation','### 4.7 Climate information also benefits weekly aggregation')

transfer='''The four additional domains broaden the geographical comparison (Fig. 5; Supplementary Table S16). H1 minus BU-rain is −0.611 mm in NW, +0.044 mm in CA, −0.001 mm in EC and −0.136 mm in TAS. The NW difference has a descriptive interval of −0.939 to −0.288 mm and is favourable in sixteen years. Its climate contrast against H0 is smaller, −0.297 mm (−0.604 to −0.010 mm). Climate contrasts in the other added domains include zero. Across these four domains, H1 improves on BU-clim by 1.32% of raw CRPS (95% interval 0.65–1.96%), while H1 minus H0 is −0.26% (−0.77 to 0.23%). Resampling whole year vectors retains spatial dependence; these four boxes are not independent samples of continental coverage.

ACCESS-S2 gives 28-day CRPS of 21.875 mm for raw, 21.606 mm for BU-rain, 21.095 mm for BU-clim, 20.847 mm for H0 and 20.823 mm for H1. H1 minus H0 is −0.025 mm (−1.154 to 1.428 mm). The separately specified summer contrast is +0.002 mm (−1.964 to 2.252 mm), so this check does not reproduce a clear northern climate-information effect. H1 minus BU-clim is −0.273 mm (−1.557 to 1.052 mm). The monthly sample provides only three summer origins per year, and the historical climate years overlap those used for ECMWF (Supplementary Table S17).

### 4.10 Simple pooling is more competitive than state-dependent weighting

Across the four primary domains, state-dependent mixing reduces 28-day CRPS relative to BU-clim by 1.222% of raw CRPS (95% interval −2.026 to −0.329% for mixture minus BU-clim; Holm-adjusted p = 0.028). Relative to learned constant weights, however, the difference is +0.318% (−0.037 to 0.692%; adjusted p = 0.117). Only SW has a favourable point estimate in that comparison. Thus the improvement over one component does not establish value from conditioning the weights on climate state (Table 6; Fig. 10).

Northern cross-validation scores are 20.277 mm for constant mixing and 20.427 mm for state-dependent mixing, compared with 20.546 mm for H1 and 20.723 mm for BU-clim. In the forward check, constant mixing scores 17.697 mm, state-dependent mixing 18.096 mm and raw ECMWF 17.847 mm. The constant-minus-raw difference is −0.149 mm (−0.924 to 0.625 mm); the state-minus-constant difference is +0.399 mm (0.105–0.649 mm). Fixed mixing therefore remains competitive, whereas the more flexible rule loses skill in this northern period. Selecting a single candidate by inner seasonal score also fails to improve consistently. Supplementary Tables S19–S21 report every candidate, rolling period, probability score and decision curve.'''
s=s.replace('EXTENSION_RESULTS_V4',transfer)
s=s.replace('EXTENSION_DISCUSSION_V4','''The geographical extension supports useful calibrated forecasts in additional settings, but a comparison with raw forecasts cannot isolate the climate contribution. H1 improves on BU-clim in the added domains on average, while its contrast with H0 remains uncertain. In ACCESS-S2, both the annual and summer H1-minus-H0 differences are close to zero. A common regional climate setting therefore does not ensure a common incremental climate benefit across forecast systems. Member count, origin frequency, spatial support and the skill already present in the dynamical forecast may all affect that benefit.

The mixture experiment provides a direct test of whether forecast state helps choose among the competing information pathways. State-dependent pooling improves on BU-clim but not on constant pooling across the primary domains. In the northern forward period it is worse than the constant mixture. Forecast combination can hedge the differences between coherent distributions without requiring a reliable conditional ranking of them. A climate coefficient or a changing mixture weight is therefore insufficient evidence of a useful meteorological decision rule. These results do not support progressing to selected circulation composites as if a stable regime rule had already been established.''')
s=s.replace('Table 7 summarises the evidence and its strength.','Table 7 summarises the evidence and its strength.')
s=s.replace('all calibrated alternatives during 2015 to 2019','the individual calibrated alternatives during 2015 to 2019').replace('every calibrated alternative remains worse','every individual calibrated alternative remains worse').replace('Raw northern ECMWF outperforms the calibrated alternatives','Raw northern ECMWF outperforms the individual calibrated alternatives')
s=s.replace('EXTENSION_CONCLUSION_V4','''The additional western Kimberley domain retains a favourable aggregate comparison, but climate contrasts are not consistently reproduced across the other domains or ACCESS-S2. Pooling coherent distributions can improve on a single weekly-aggregation component. State-dependent weights do not improve on constant weights across the primary domains, and are less effective in the northern forward check. The practical result is a hierarchy of comparisons to make, rather than a demonstrated rule for always choosing reconciliation.''')
s=s.replace('Temporal consistency is an accounting property; its predictive benefit must be established for the forecast system, location and application period.','Temporal consistency provides compatible products across horizons. Whether calibration, reconciliation or forecast combination adds predictive value remains specific to the system, location and application period.')
s=s.replace('''## Acknowledgements and use of AI\n\n''','''## Acknowledgements and use of AI\n\n''')
replace('## Acknowledgements and use of AI','## References','The authors used OpenAI Codex for coding, analysis support and language editing, and take responsibility for the final manuscript.')
# Stable method names throughout prose; mathematical H0/H1 definitions remain intact.
for old,new in [('rainfall-only bottom-up','BU-rain'),('Rainfall-only bottom-up','BU-rain'),('climate-informed weekly aggregation','BU-clim'),('Climate-informed weekly aggregation','BU-clim')]:s=s.replace(old,new)
s=s.replace('There are two ways to produce coherent forecasts after calibration. Bottom-up aggregation sums the calibrated weekly samples.','There are two ways to produce coherent forecasts after calibration. Bottom-up aggregation sums the calibrated weekly samples; we distinguish rainfall-only weekly calibration (BU-rain) from climate-informed weekly calibration (BU-clim).')
s=s.replace('EXTENSION_ABSTRACT_V4','')
